          "passage_id": "gkr6-p-055",
          "locator": "Krīḍā 55.20–29; https://sanskritdocuments.org/doc_purana/gaNeshapurANam2.html",
          "paraphrase": "Śukla returns dejectedly with his wife, wondering whether the poor food offended the Lord. Missing the leaf hut, he becomes anxious. Servants massage, bathe and adorn both with fragrant oils, ornaments and gold garments; they behold gem/gold walls, beds/seats, pearl/gem designs, golden vessels, coverings and abundant foods. They wonder how the little dwelling became like Indra’s house. Śukla tells his wife this is Vināyaka’s grace, given unobtrusively: the Lord treats his own abundant gifts as little and a small devoted offering as great. He recommends remembering, bowing, praising and worshipping, through fear, desire, affection or even hostility, for welfare.",
